That posting sums up the most important points. Some additions:

* Drag your pet around with a leash. Effectively, that teleports it
next to you (unless the leash snaps loose harmlessly and you have to
go re-leash it, small chance). You can even transport it through
closed doors and walls. It will screach, yowl, bark etc, but that's
just cosmetic.

* Light sources help you see where the pet is, but do not influence
pet behavior.

* A tin whistle or a bell acts largely like a leash for 5 turns on all
pets within the affected area. That's a circle with radius
Sqrt(20*ulevel) centered on you. You are virtually guaranteed to find
a whistle on the mine town watch. The downside is waking up nymphs.

* Some other actions have the same effect as a tin whistle: kicking
(even objects, walls, or empty air; unless kicked monster evades),
cursed magic whistle, non-blessed eucalyptus leaf, digging down on a
water/lava, digging into iron bars, forcing a lock with a blunt
implement, noise from fumbling, squeaky board trap, exploding chest/
door traps and land mines. I think throwing things also has a tin
whistle effect, but I can't find it in the source.

* A magic whistle or a blessed eucalyptus leaf is the only real
solution. As a pacifist, it's worth doing the mines for that reason
alone. Kick the trees in mine town - you are guaranteed some fruit,
which can be eucalyptus. Even with a magic whistle, it's a good idea
to keep one pet leashed in case you step on a trap door yourself. As
an aside, you can whistle your pet into the castle if you levitate
over the moat between the towers and the edge of the map.

* As soon as you find and can use a polymorph trap, get a faster,
better pet. Try to zap it with speed monster while it is in a non-
magic resistant form, and to feed it (meatballs...) while it eats.
Splitting pet puddings is tempting, but that counts as abuse. If you
have a pet chameleon, you need a ring of protection from shape
changers to morph it into something more useful.


